# Large synthetic query data is tracked in LFS; the curated benchmark is not.
#
# Scoped by path rather than by `*.jsonl`, deliberately. The four files under
# evals/retrieval/benchmark/ are 5-39 KB of hand-curated gold data that belong in
# plain git, and an extension-wide rule silently converts them to LFS pointers on
# the next touch -- adding an LFS dependency to anyone who just wants to run evals.
#
# Only the generated sets go here. They are the one pipeline artifact that cannot
# be regenerated: chunk corpora are deterministic, because index_contract.py pins
# INDEX_CHUNKER_VERSION and re-indexing the same HTML reproduces byte-identical
# chunk IDs, but generated queries are LLM samples. The seed makes them
# repeatable on one GPU; CUDA kernel nondeterminism and a different accelerator
# (MLX on Apple Silicon) do not reproduce them at all.
#
# LFS rather than plain git because GitHub hard-blocks any file over 100 MB and
# the deduplicated column corpus is 153 MB.
# Only the bulk JSONL. The *_report.json files beside them are a few KB of
# hashes and counts, and those are the most useful thing to read in a diff --
# an LFS pointer would hide exactly the provenance a reviewer wants to check.
evals/retrieval/synthetic/*.jsonl filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
evals/retrieval/synthetic/**/*.jsonl filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.faiss filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
